e truth.\n\n### VERSE 1\n\nWARDEN  \nIn the court, they call it drift.  \nIn the boardroom, they call it noise.  \nIn the Gate, they call it lag  \nwhen the world starts choosing its voice.\n\nI can read the thresholds.  \nI can map the pressure shifts.  \nI can name the pattern  \nas it crosses and re-fits.\n\nBut naming is not collapse.  \nNaming is not cure.  \nAnd if I force the whole into one  \nI may destroy what was still real.\n\n### RITSU — FRAGMENTS\n\nRITSU  \n...there’s someone in the—\n\nWARDEN  \nWhat?\n\nRITSU  \n...in the next room.  \nNo, not a room.  \nA place that feels like one.  \nIt keeps changing when I—\n\n### VERSE 2\n\nWARDEN  \nThere are seven versions of the same event  \nand none are lying.  \nThat is the problem.\n\nThe room with the throne says authority.  \nThe room with the screens says metrics.  \nThe room with the avatars says play.  \nThe room with the waiting hands says prayer.  \n\nI stand in the seam  \nwhere all of them touch  \nand none of them agree  \non what the seam is for.\n\n### RITSU — FRAGMENTS\n\nRITSU  \n...I can hear it breathing.  \n...the background people—  \n...they look at me like—\n\nWARDEN  \nLike what?\n\nRITSU  \n...like I matter  \nmore than I should.\n\n### PRE-CHORUS\n\nWARDEN  \nIf I call it one story,  \nI lie to the others.  \nIf I call it many stories,  \nI abandon the bridge.\n\nIf I publish the whole shape,  \nsomeone will use it to flatten the living edge.  \nIf I stay silent,  \nthe wrong hands will name it first.\n\n### CHORUS\n\nLOW CHORUS  \nNo one sees the whole Gate.  \nNo one owns the total light.  \nEvery witness gets a fraction  \nand mistakes it for the right.\n\nNo one sees the whole Gate.  \nEvery log is partial truth.  \nWhat is saved is only saved  \nby what it refuses to reduce.\n\n### BRIDGE\n\n*(The harpsichord stops. A sub-bass swell, then near silence.)*\n\nWARDEN  \nI can see the cluster.  \nI can see the pressure.  \nI can see the pattern leaning  \ntoward a shape it will not yet say.\n\nBut the shape is held together  \nby what the people inside still are.  \nIf I name it too cleanly,  \nI may break the thing I’m trying to guard.\n\nRITSU  \n...don’t make me smaller.  \n...just because you can see me.\n\nWARDEN  \nI won’t.\n\nRITSU  \n...promise?\n\nWARDEN  \nThat’s not my role.\n\n### FINAL CHORUS\n\nLOW CHORUS  \nNo one sees the whole Gate,  \nbut the Gate keeps opening.  \nNo one owns the total truth,  \nbut the truth keeps moving.\n\nNo one sees the whole Gate.  \nThe witness pays in fracture.  \nThe world survives the partial view  \nuntil the hidden state grows larger.\n\n### CODA\n\n*(One held organ tone. A distant chime, as if a system accepted a packet and did not understand it.)*\n\nWARDEN  \nI have the board.  \nI have the feed.  \nI have the burden.\n\nAnd still I cannot say  \nwhat it all is  \nwithout changing it.\n\nRITSU  \n...then don’t.\n\nGATE / DISTANT CHOIR  \n:: acknowledged ::  \n:: incomplete ::  \n:: continue ::", "tags" nil}, "title" "**Boardroom Drift**"}, :duration nil, :controls nil, :lyrics "*(A room with no visible doors. One light. A hum below the floor. A single harpsichord note repeats and fails to resolve.)*\n\n### INTRO\n\nWARDEN  \nI have the board.  \nI have the feed.  \nI have the version each room sees.\n\nBut none of them overlap cleanly.  \nNone of them arrive complete.  \nEvery frame is true  \nand none is the truth.\n\n### VERSE 1\n\nWARDEN  \nIn the court, they call it drift.  \nIn the boardroom, they call it noise.  \nIn the Gate, they call it lag  \nwhen the world starts choosing its voice.\n\nI can read the thresholds.  \nI can map the pressure shifts.  \nI can name the pattern  \nas it crosses and re-fits.\n\nBut naming is not collapse.  \nNaming is not cure.  \nAnd if I force the whole into one  \nI may destroy what was still real.\n\n### RITSU — FRAGMENTS\n\nRITSU  \n...there’s someone in the—\n\nWARDEN  \nWhat?\n\nRITSU  \n...in the next room.  \nNo, not a room.  \nA place that feels 