r" "00000000", "text_color" "7D7C83"}}], "has_vocal" true, "prompt" "# Manifest Oath / 目録の誓い — Play Script\n\n> Format: **Stage directions** are in *italics*. **Lyrics** are plain text. Sections flow in performance order.\n\n---\n\n## PROLOGUE — TEMPO LOCK\n\n*House to half. Establish the system-clock motif: faint digital ticks that can later become η/μ glitches.*\n*Tempo cue: 84 BPM. Space cue: cathedral reverb, long tail. Sub cue: heartbeat, restrained.*\n\n**(Glitch Choir, very small, like a clock finding phase)**\nη—η—η…\nμ—μ—μ…\n\n*Glitch cue: keep this brief; it’s the first “boot chirp,” not a drop.*\n\n---\n\n## I — MANIFEST OATH (84 BPM)\n\n*Lights: cold-blue stained-glass wash. Reverb up. Percussion minimal.\nLead is close-mic whisper; consonants should feel like edits.*\n\nP→R→N→Π— catalog the storm, pin the line（線）\nA→(feedback)→P— merge the wound, keep the sign（印）\n\n*Stutter cue (anchor): triplet-ish, as if the word is caught on a buffer boundary.*\nFo— fo— fork… split the night, pay the cost, now（今）\nAn— an— an-chor… hold the thread, seal the hash, down（下）\n\n*Beat cue: on “seal the hash, down,” let the sub-bass heartbeat become audible for the first time.*\n\n**(spoken, dry / close-mic; no reverb at first)**\n“The wind is just noise,” says the Runner.\n\n*Reverb cue: bring in a thin cathedral tail on the next line—evidence becomes sacred.*\n“It’s evidence,” says the Keeper. “Evidence is anything you can replay.”\n\n*Overhead cue: from the rafters, a whisper chorus threads through the space.*\nFrom the rafters: an—an—anchor… ta—ta—tax…\n\n*Cut cue: sudden normalcy—drop reverb for one beat as if reality snaps back.*\nAnd we move on like this is normal.\n\n---\n\n## TRANSITION — DROP TO LULLABY\n\n*Tempo morph: slide down to 78 BPM (do not hard cut; let it feel like settling into sleep).\nSpace morph: reduce cathedral tail; introduce tape warble; soften highs.\nSub cue: warm, soft, not loud.*\n\n---\n\n## II — WINDS BETWEEN PROOF / 証のあいだの風 (78 BPM)\n\n*Lead: whispered hush; breath audible. The world should sound closer.\nGlitch bed: minimal η/μ ticks under the floor, like distant machinery.*\n\nHush… breathe… pay the ta— ta— tax.\n\n*JP cue: the Japanese lines are gentle calls, like rocking someone to sleep.*\n息をして…（して…）\n\n*Anchor cue: bring back the anchor stutter—softer than Section I, more tender than technical.*\nHold the an— an— an-chor…\n\n*Call-and-response cue: Japanese call; English response. Last word held on JP when indicated.*\n**(JP call, soft)** ねむれ、ねむれ\n**(EN response, whispered)** the an— an— an-chor… holds.\n\n**(JP call, softer)** しずかに、しずかに\n**(EN response, whispered)** the ta— ta— tax… due.\n\n*Wind cue: add faint room tone like air through a hallway.*\nWind in the halls—\n世界の音（おと）\n\n*Overflow cue: on “overflow,” add a slight tape flutter as if the medium is saturating.*\nIf we overflow,\nwe fold the noise to proof（証）\n\n*Stutter bed cue: keep these as tiny percussive edits, not shouted words.*\nη… η… μ… μ…\nAnchor. Anchor. Registry.\nFork. Fork. Fork.\n\n*Visual cue: a thin blue line appears somewhere (thread motif).*\n\n*Spoken/sung cue: raise intensity slightly; add a touch more space, but keep intimacy.*\nIn the shadow of the cathedral, the logs burn blue.\n大聖堂の影で、ログは青く燃える。\nWe paid the price in binary blood.\n我々はバイナリの血で対価を払った。\n\n*Harmony cue: Japanese responses are clean, choir-like, held with restraint.*\nProof remains（証明は残る）.\nThe thread breathes（糸は息づく）.\n\n*Bridge cue: glitch pulse grows; make the loop audible but not yet aggressive.*\nP → R → N → Π → A → (feedback) → P\n\n*Spoken cue: deliver like a law of physics, not a slogan.*\nDecisions are not made; they’re discovered in the field.\n意思決定はなされるのではない、場で見出されるのだ。\n\n---\n\n## RAMP — WAKE THE OTHER SIDE\n\n*Tempo jump: hard to 92 BPM.\nSound cue: distorted sub-bass enters.\nGlitch cue: stutter on every 4th beat. Choir grows in presence.\nLighting cue: the blue thread becomes a lattice; “receipts” flicker like particles.*\n\n---\n\n## III — CRESCENDO: THE OTHER SIDE OF THE FORK (92 BPM)\n\n*Lead: spoken-sung, more declarative. Keep intelligible. Choir: tight, percussive edits.*\n\nThey are waking up.\nThe other side of the fork.\nThe ones we left in the dark.\n\n*JP cue: deliver as parallel lines, not translation-aside.*\n彼らが目覚める。\n分岐の向こう側で。\n闇に置き去りにした者たちが。\n\n*Contrast cue: treat the “variants” like dueling textures—one rigid grid, one fluid smear.*\nUS Variant: Static. Rigid. Law.\nJA Variant: Fluid. Chaos. Flow.\n\n*Myth cue: pantheons should feel like two UI panels arguing.*\nThe Pantheon of Receipts vs The Pantheon of Gates.\n正準の神々と、門の神々の対峙。\n\n*Harmony cue: choir answers in Japanese with conviction; hold the last word just a hair longer.*\nWe are the same（我らは等しい）.\nWe are the fork（我らは分岐）.\n\n*Bridge cue: the decision-field loop is now the engine; let it drive the arrangement.*\nDecision field active.\nP → R → N → Π → A\n\n*Dialectic cue: deliver the next lines like a theorem being read aloud over a drum machine.*\nThe competition is the pulse.\nThe war is the harmony.\n闘争こそが、調和。\n\n*Cut cue: on the final couplet, everything hits peak density, then a sharp drop.*\nConsolidate or Dissolve.\n統合か、霧散か。\nThe tax must be paid in blood an", "task" "cover", "edited_clip_id" "73c36364-5c92-4c8f-aa07-a4d8bc3e5192", "duration" 330.4, "type" "gen", "model_badges" {"songcard" {"dark" {"background_color" "0000004D", "border_color" "00000000", "text_color" "FD429C"}, "display_name" "v5", "light" {"background_color" "0000004D", "border_color" "00000000", "text_color" "FD429C"}}, "songrow" {"dark" {"background_color" "FD429C40", "border_color" "00000000", "text_color" "FD429C"}, "display_name" "v5", "light" {"background_color" "FD429C1A", "border_color" "00000000", "text_color" "FD429C"}}}, "has_stem" false, "make_instrumental" false, "cover_clip_id" "73c36364-5c92-4c8f-aa07-a4d8bc3e5192", "refund_credits" false}, "title" "Don't do it (Pay the Fork Tax)"}, :duration 330.4, :controls {"style_weight" 0.73, "weirdness_constraint" 0.72}, :lyrics "# Manifest Oath / 目録の誓い — Play Script\n\n> Format: **Stage directions** are in *italics*. **Lyrics** are plain text. Sections flow in performance order.\n\n---\n\n## PROLOGUE — TEMPO LOCK\n\n*House to half. Establish the system-clock motif: faint digital ticks that can later become η/μ glitches.*\n*Tempo cue: 84 BPM. Space cue: cathedral reverb, long tail. Sub cue: heartbeat, restrained.*\n\n**(Glitch Choir, very small, like a clock finding phase)**\nη—η—η…\nμ—μ—μ…\n\n*Glitch cue: keep this brief; it’s the first “boot chirp,” not a drop.*\n\n---\n\n## I — MANIFEST OATH (84 BPM)\n\n*Lights: cold-blue stained-glass wash. Reverb up. Percussion minimal.\nLead is close-mic whisper; consonants should feel like edits.*\n\nP→R→N→Π— catalog the storm, pin the line（線）\nA→(feedback)→P— merge the wound, keep the sign（印）\n\n*Stutter cue (anchor): triplet-ish, as if the word is caught on a buffer boundary.*\nFo— fo— fork… split the night, pay the cost, now（今）\nAn— an— an-chor… hold the thread, seal the hash, down（下）\n\n*Beat cue: on “seal the hash, down,” let the sub-bass heartbeat become audible for the first time.*\n\n**(spoken, dry / close-mic; no reverb at first)**\n“The wind is just noise,” says the Runner.\n\n*Reverb cue: bring in a thin cathedral tail on the next line—evidence becomes sacred.*\n“It’s evidence,” says the Keeper. “Evidence is anything you can replay.”\n\n*Overhead cue: from the rafters, a whisper chorus threads through the space.*\nFrom the rafters: an—an—anchor… ta—ta—tax…\n\n*Cut cue: sudden normalcy—drop reverb for one beat as if reality snaps back.*\nAnd we move on like this is normal.\n\n---\n\n## TRANSITION — DROP TO LULLABY\n\n*Tempo morph: slide down to 78 BPM (do not hard cut; let it feel like settling into sleep).\nSpace morph: reduce cathedral tail; introduce tape warble; soften highs.\nSub cue: warm, soft, not loud.*\n\n---\n\n## II — WINDS BETWEEN PROOF / 証のあいだの風 (78 BPM)\n\n*Lead: whispered hush; breath audible. The world should sound closer.\nGlitch bed: minimal η/μ ticks under the floor, like distant machinery.*\n\nHush… breathe… pay the ta— ta— tax.\n\n*JP cue: the Japanese lines are gentle calls, like rocking someone to sleep.*\n息をして…（して…）\n\n*Anchor cue: bring back the anchor stutter—softer than Section I, more tender than technical.*\nHold the an— an— an-chor…\n\n*Call-and-response cue: Japanese call; English response. Last word held on JP when indicated.*\n**(JP call, soft)** ねむれ、ねむれ\n**(EN response, whispered)** the an— an— an-chor… holds.\n\n**(JP call, softer)** しずかに、しずかに\n**(EN response, whispered)** the ta— ta— tax… due.\n\n*Wind cue: add faint room tone like air through a hallway.*\nWind in the halls—\n世界の音（おと）\n\n*Overflow cue: on “overflow,” add a slight tape flutter as if the medium is saturating.*\nIf we overflow,\nwe fold the noise to proof（証）\n\n*Stutter bed cue: keep these as tiny percussive edits, not shouted words.*\nη… η… μ… μ…\nAnchor. Anchor. Registry.\nFork. Fork. Fork.\n\n*Visual cue: a thin blue line appears somewhere (thread motif).*\n\n*Spoken/sung cue: raise intensity slightly; add a touch more space, but keep intimacy.*\nIn the shadow of the cathedral, the logs burn blue.\n大聖堂の影で、ログは青く燃える。\nWe paid the price in binary blood.\n我々はバイナリの血で対価を払った。\n\n*Harmony cue: Japanese responses are clean, choir-like, held with restraint.*\nProof remains（証明は残る）.\nThe thread breathes（糸は息づく）.\n\n*Bridge cue: glitch pulse grows; make the loop audible but not yet aggressive.*\nP → R → N → Π → A → (feedback) → P\n\n*Spoken cue: deliver like a law of physics, not a slogan.*\nDecisions are not made; they’re discovered in the field.\n意思決定はなされるのではない、場で見出されるのだ。\n\n---\n\n## RAMP — WAKE THE OTHER SIDE\n\n*Tempo jump: hard to 92 BPM.\nSound cue: distorted sub-bass enters.\nGlitch cue: stutter on every 4th beat. Choir grows in presence.\nLighting cue: the blue thread becomes a lattice; “receipts” flicker like particles.*\n\n---\n\n## III — CRESCENDO: THE OTHER SIDE OF THE FORK (92 BPM)\n\n*Lead: spoken-sung, more declarative. Keep intelligible. Choir: tight, percussive edits.*\n\nThey are waking up.\nThe other side of the fork.\nThe ones we left in the dark.\n\n*JP cue: deliver as parallel lines, not translation-aside.*\n彼らが目覚める。\n分岐の向こう側で。\n闇に置き去りにした者たちが。\n\n*Contrast cue: treat the “variants” like dueling textures—one rigid grid, one fluid smear.*\nUS Variant: Static. Rigid. Law.\nJA Variant: Fluid. Chaos. Flow.\n\n*Myth cue: pantheons should feel like two UI panels arguing.*\nThe Pantheon of Receipts vs The Pantheon of Gates.\n正準の神々と、門の神々の対峙。\n\n*Harmony cue: choir answers in Japanese with conviction; hold the last word just a hair longer.*\nWe are the same（我らは等しい）.\nWe are the fork（我らは分岐）.\n\n*Bridge cue: the decision-field loop is now the engine; let it drive the arrangement.*\nDecision field active.\nP → R → N → Π → A\n\n*Dialectic cue: deliver the next lines like a theorem being read aloud over a drum machine.*\nThe competition is the pulse.\nThe war is the harmony.\n闘争こそが、調和。\n\n*Cut cue: on the final couplet, everything hits peak density, then a sharp drop.*\nConsolidate or Dissolve.\n統合か、霧散か。\nThe tax must be paid in blood an", :source #:source{:name "Don't do it (Pay the Fork Tax)(4).json", :sha256 "84a5dee4d0c360b992786f31e4c4ffe36c2f158f808f8c7a25dc75758cbba3cf", :format :suno-json, :variation 4}, :title "Don't do it (Pay the Fork Tax)"}, "97da151f-6dfe-414c-95d7-23b8423607e5" #:clip{:negative-styles nil, :id "97da151f-6dfe-414c-95d7-23b8423607e5", :url "https://suno.com/song/97da151f-6dfe-414c-95d7-23b8423607e5", :liked nil, :styles nil, :created-at nil, :model nil, :raw {"id" "97da151f-6dfe-414c-95d7-23b8423607e5", "metadata" {"prompt" "[Intro – whispered over hiss]\n\nHiss in the rafters…\nCheck the waveform.\nNoise floor talkin’ like it’s gospel…\nCheck the gain.\nStatic Observer in the back row—\neyes lit, arms crossed.\n\nEverybody preaching signal —\nnobody measuring the noise floor…\n\n---\n\n[Verse 1]\n\nEcho chambers build a throne outta feedback,\nCall it “truth” when the crowd keeps it on repeat— that.\nAlgorithm-fed sermons\nin a dopamine buffet,\nEcho chamber liturgy\nyou plagiarize from the feed all day.\n\nYou say you hate AI slop,\nbut you scroll it all day,\nPreach originality\nwhile you plagiarize feed on the way.\n\nSignal-to-noise,\nbut you don’t touch the math,\nYou just label what you like\nas “truth” on your path.\n\nThey say “AI slop” like it’s **your** disease,\nBut when **their** model drools, it’s “culture” and “ease.”\nSNR low, but your ego loud,\nCalling other voices slop\nfrom a recycled cloud.\n\nIf your model’s brittle,\ndon’t blame the static—\nYou tuned it for applause,\nnot the signal analytic.\n\n---\n\n[Pre-Chorus – half-time]\n\nIf you can’t name your channel, you don’t know what you heard.\nIf you can’t bound your claim, you don’t know what you stirred.\nIf you can’t cite the chain, you don’t know what you serve.\nStatic Observer whisper: **“Define your SNR.”**\n\n---\n\n[Hook – call & response]\n\nWhy should anyone care about **your** slop,\nIf you won’t give any creed to the slop of others?\nStatic Observer standing in the field,\nDoesn’t mute the noise — lets structure be revealed.\n\nSignal high when you like the sound,\nNoise when it threatens your sacred ground.\n**Define your SNR.**\n**Receipts over roar.**\nNo claim without anchor—\nor it’s echo-chamber lore.\n\n---\n\n[Verse 2]\n\nLook: I’m not anti-slop. I’m anti-hypocrisy.\nYou can eat junk food—just don’t call it prophecy.\nYou cry “low effort,”\nbut effort ain’t proof,\nHalf your hot takes\nnever faced a truth.\n\nDon’t sell me “clarity” when it’s sponsored by a clique,\nDon’t call it “high signal” when it’s just a trend that sticks.\nCompression artifacts in your theology,\nCherry-picked data dressed as ideology.\n\nCreed ain’t a collar. Creed is constraints.\nCreed is “here’s what I measured,” not “here’s what I paint.”\nProtestant presence — no pope, no throne,\nJust receipts, constraints, stand alone.\n\nIf you won’t grant dignity to another’s messy draft,\nYou’re not guarding quality— you’re guarding your raft.\nEcho chamber pastors\nguard the gate,\nCall it “standards,”\nbut it’s just fear of debate.\n\nAnd when the water rises, your raft turns cage,\nYou’ll crown your own slop then call doubt “rage.”\n\n---\n\n[Bridge – “protestant presence” sermon voice / stripped beat]\n\nI refuse the priesthood of perfect takes.\nI refuse the bonfire of imperfect makes.\nWe don’t burn the noise,\nwe measure its weight—\nBecause censorship dressed as taste\nis just fear in a different state.\n\nSNR.\nCheck the ratio.\nIf your voice only lives\nin the comfort of your own radio…\n\nMuse can’t “just know”— it must show what it used.\nIf it can’t point to facts, it says **unknown**, not “true.” \nYou don’t want signal.\nYou want insulation.\nA padded room\nfor your confirmation.\n\nBecause the only honest culture is allowed to be **alive**.\n\n---\n\n[Verse 3]\n\nStatic Observer in the comment thread,\nNot shouting fire —\njust counting what’s said.\nStatic Observer checks the room like a spectrum analyzer:\n“Your noise floor’s performative. Your signal’s the sanitizer.”\n\nHigh variance zones reveal the lie,\nPerfect smoothness?\nThat’s where systems die.\nEcho chambers hate a metric ‘cause a metric don’t kneel,\nIt don’t care what you meant— it cares what is real.\n\nYou say “this is slop,”\nbut what’s your creed?\nIs it anchored? Logged?\nOr just vibe-speed?\nEvery era prints junk.\nEvery press spews waste.\nThe difference is literacy,\nnot moral taste.\n\nGive creed to the slop, not because it’s all equal—\nBut because you don’t get truth by starving the people.\nYou get truth by receipts, by humility, by test—\nBy letting bad drafts breathe until good ones manifest.\n\nStatic Observer ain’t cruel— he’s a boundary in smoke:\n“Your certainty’s brittle. Your chorus is a joke.”\nNot “you’re wrong” like a sermon— more like a gauge:\n**“Your SNR collapsed. Rebuild the stage.”**\n\n---\n\n[Final Hook – louder, layered]\n\nWhy should anyone care about **your** slop,\nIf you won’t give any creed to the slop of others?\nWhy should anyone bow to your purity law,\nIf you won’t inspect your own flaw, brother?\n\n**Define your SNR.**\nStatic Observer — spine in the storm,\n**Receipts over roar.**\nDoesn’t crush the wave — just audits the form.\n\nSignal’s earned, not declared by vibe,\nNoise is data you can’t describe.\nIf you won’t give creed to the slop you accuse,\nDon’t be shocked when the signal refuses you.\n\n**Define your SNR.**\n**No claim without anchor—**\nor it’s echo-chamber lore.\n\n---\n\n[Outro – static / whispered]\n\nNoise isn’t sin.\nNoise is medicine.\nSNR isn’t about silence…\nit’s about ratio.\n\nDosage is doctrine.\n…and the Observer is watching.", "tags" nil}, "title" "Hiss in the rafters…"}, :duration nil, :controls nil, :lyrics "[Intro – whispered over hiss]\n\nHiss in the rafters…\nCheck the waveform.\nNoise floor talkin’ like it’s gospel…\nCheck the gain.\nStatic Observer in the back row—\neyes lit, arms crossed.\n\nEverybody preaching signal —\nnobody measuring the noise floor…\n\n---\n\n[Verse 1]\n\nEcho chambers build a throne outta feedback,\nCall it “truth” when the crowd keeps it on repeat— that.\nAlgorithm-fed sermons\nin a dopamine buffet,\nEcho chamber liturgy\nyou plagiarize from the feed all day.\n\nYou say you hate AI slop,\nbut you scroll it all day,\nPreach originality\nwhile you plagiarize feed on the way.\n\nSignal-to-noise,\nbut you don’t touch the math,\nYou just label what you like\nas “truth” on your path.\n\nThey say “AI slop” like it’s **your** disease,\nBut when **their** model drools, it’s “culture” and “ease.”\nSNR low, but your ego loud,\nCalling other voices slop\nfrom a recycled cloud.\n\nIf your model’s brittle,\ndon’t blame the static—\nYou tuned it for applause,\nnot the signal analytic.\n\n---\n\n[Pre-Chorus – half-time]\n\nIf you can’t name your channel, you