le\nSoprano royal court. A world that places itself above the people it governs.\n\n## Style Prompt\nHigh soprano, harpsichord flurries, ceremonial drums, organ pedal, restrained brass warnings, polished authority, calm intensity, rigid phrasing, frame shifts through sudden cuts, no heroic uplift.\n\n## Excluded Style\nNo EDM drop, no trailer braams, no tavern bounce, no pop-ballad belting, no symphonic metal, no glossy neon, no soft haze, no triumphant snare march.\n\n## Lyrics\n### Verse I\n\n#### Court\nI was taught the ceiling was the sky\nAnd every rung below it ohousand little weather systems under reign\n\n#### Corridor of Soft Treason\n\nA cook knows hunger\nA clerk knows names\nA guard looks downward\nAnd leaves with stains\n\n#### City\n\nThe city hears through brick and steam\nThrough broken sleep and market screams\nThrough alley prayer and factory cough\nThrough all the names the rich rubbed off\n\n### Verse II\n\n#### Court\nEvery bell confirms what law has named\nEvery carpet hides the ash of blame\nFrom the gallery the city looks like grain\nA thousand little weather systems under reign\n\n#### City\nSome carry bread and some carry debt\nSome carry all the things they cannot get\nSome hold a room with six inside\nSome hold a key and still have nowhere safe to hide\n\n\n### Pre-Chorus\nIf the street is burning\nClose the velvet door\nIf the crowd is singing\nCount the silver more\n\n### Section\nSoft treason travels\nHand over hand\nNot through the anthem\nBut through what was planned\n\n### Chorus\nWe are the hand above the flood\nWe are the seal upon the blood\nWe name the hour\nWe bless the chain\nAnd call the distance sacred when it looks like pain\n\n### Refrain\nThrough the service hall\nThrough the papers thin\nWhat the court keeps out\nStill finds a way in\n\n### Verse III\n\n#### Court\n\nStill there are those who read beyond the glass\nWho hear the kitchen rumors as they pass\nWho know the map is faithful only here\nAnd every polished answer has been cut by fear\n\n#### City\n\n### Bridge\n\nLow voices in the corridor:\nThere is a ledger under every hymn\nThere is a kingdom hanging by a whim\nThere is a child below who learned our names\nBy watching where the carriage wheels made stains\n\n### Final Chorus\nWe are the hand above the flood\nWe are the seal upon the blood\nBut every wall that keeps us pure\nMakes every truth outside the court less easy to ignore\n\n### Outro\nHarpsichord alone.\nA bell.\nBreath held too long.", "tags" nil}, "title" "Organ-Pedal Oath"}, :duration nil, :controls nil, :lyrics "# The Court Above the Street\n\n## Role\nSoprano royal court. A world that places itself above the people it governs.\n\n## Style Prompt\nHigh soprano, harpsichord flurries, ceremonial drums, organ pedal, restrained brass warnings, polished authority, calm intensity, rigid phrasing, frame shifts through sudden cuts, no heroic uplift.\n\n## Excluded Style\nNo EDM drop, no trailer braams, no tavern bounce, no pop-ballad belting, no symphonic metal, no glossy neon, no soft haze, no triumphant snare march.\n\n## Lyrics\n### Verse I\n\n#### Court\nI was taught the ceiling was the sky\nAnd every rung below it ohousand little weather systems under reign\n\n#### Corridor of Soft Treason\n\nA cook knows hunger\nA clerk knows names\nA guard looks downward\nAnd leaves with stains\n\n#### City\n\nThe city hears through brick and steam\nThrough broken sleep and market screams\nThrough alley prayer and factory cough\nThrough all the names the rich rubbed off\n\n### Verse II\n\n#### Court\nEvery bell confirms what law has named\nEvery carpet hides the ash of blame\nFrom the gallery the city looks like grain\nA thousand little weather systems under reign\n\n#### City\nSome carry bread and some carry debt\nSome carry all the things they cannot get\nSome hold a room with six inside\nSome hold a key and still have nowhere safe to hide\n\n\n### Pre-Chorus\nIf the street is burning\nClose the velvet door\nIf the crowd is 