ops\nIt never begins\nIt just is\nAnd it always will be\nA mind is a terrible thing to waste\nBut words are cheap\nTo be afraid to speak them\nIs to be afraid to use the mind\nAnd that is the waste\nInk stains on a carpet\nLeaking from wounds which have no scar", :source #:source{:name "Beginner's Mind.txt", :sha256 "eaff4229db2135ac5726209e6d08aa4b671f8f96345983353a7055951a241b11", :format :suno-text, :variation nil}, :title "Beginner's Mind"}, "f5559ff2-385d-458d-970c-97b72ded4f58" #:clip{:negative-styles nil, :id "f5559ff2-385d-458d-970c-97b72ded4f58", :url "https://suno.com/song/f5559ff2-385d-458d-970c-97b72ded4f58", :liked true, :styles "mythic glitch epicspokensung delivery in the nf  Hip-hop, rap, spoken word lane\nexistential rap\na mythic synthchoral ballad with glitch undertones 84 bpm slow build cathedral reverb soft subbass like a heartbeat", :created-at nil, :model "v4.5-all", :raw {"audio_url" "https://cdn1.suno.ai/f5559ff2-385d-458d-970c-97b72ded4f58.mp3", "id" "f5559ff2-385d-458d-970c-97b72ded4f58", "image_url" "https://cdn2.suno.ai/5cb1e1c1-3666-4190-8501-cb3d3b57c1d0.jpeg", "isCover" false, "isLiked" true, "isRemaster" false, "metadata" {"uses_latest_model" false, "stream" true, "is_remix" false, "priority" 0, "tags" "mythic glitch epicspokensung delivery in the nf  Hip-hop, rap, spoken word lane\nexistential rap\na mythic synthchoral ballad with glitch undertones 84 bpm slow build cathedral reverb soft subbass like a heartbeat", "video_is_stale" false, "can_remix" true, "artist_reference_warning" {"artist_to_tag_mapping" {"watsky" ["Hip-hop" "rap" "spoken word"]}, "modified_prompt" "mythic glitch epicspokensung delivery in the nf  Hip-hop, rap, spoken word lane\nexistential rap\na mythic synthchoral ballad with glitch undertones 84 bpm slow build cathedral reverb soft subbass like a heartbeat"}, "prompt" "(84 BPM—slow build. Cathedral reverb. Sub-bass: *thum… thum…* like a heart remembering.)\n\nI used to think music was decoration—\na candle on the table of the world.\nTurns out it’s the table.\nTurns out it’s the world’s **operating system**\nwearing a choir robe and a cracked screen.\n\nListen—\nbefore fire had a name,\nbefore language learned to stand upright,\nwe had rhythm:\na metronome made of footsteps fleeing predators,\na lullaby made of breath saying *you’re still here.*\nThat’s not art.\nThat’s **survival code**.\n\nBecause sound doesn’t ask permission—\nit enters the body like a key through a lock you forgot you built.\nIt bypasses the bouncer called “thought”\nand shakes hands with the basement called “memory.”\nSo when I say “song,” I mean **tool.**\nWhen I say “chorus,” I mean **lever.**\n\nAnd the gods?\nThey knew.\n\nThey forged hymns like spears,\nthey tuned temples to frequencies that made crowds feel like one creature.\nCathedral reverb wasn’t an accident—\nit was an amplifier for awe,\na hack for the nervous system,\na way to write “together” into stone.\n\nThen the glitch arrived—\nnot as error, as revelation.\n\nStatic in the halo.\nA choir that stutters mid-amen.\nA bass pulse like a heartbeat under armor.\nAnd suddenly the sacred isn’t clean—\nit’s true.\n\nBecause truth doesn’t come polished.\nTruth comes *buffering.*\nTruth comes with artifacts.\nTruth comes with the little skips where the soul says,\n“Hold up… rewind that… I felt something.”\n\nSo I learned:\nmusic is a technology for moving **mass**—\nnot just bodies in a room,\nbut attention,\nand grief,\nand love,\nand rage,\nand the quiet decision to keep going.\n\nIt’s a way to compress a whole year of pain\ninto three minutes you can carry.\nA way to turn the unspeakable\ninto something your ribs can understand.\n\nAnd if you’ve ever had a song save your life—\ndon’t romanticize it.\nName it plainly.\n\nThat was a device\nthat rerouted your circuit\nwhen the power grid inside you went dark.\nThat was a protocol\nthat kept the signal from collapsing into noise.\nThat was a handrail built out of harmonics\nwhen the staircase disappeared.\n\nYou want myth?\n\nOkay.\n\nPrometheus didn’t steal fire.\nHe stole **tempo.**\nHe stole the click-track behind the stars.\nHe stole the chord that makes strangers sing the same note\nand call it home.\nAnd for that theft, the punishment was simple:\n\nEvery day, an eagle named Doubt\npecks at the liver named Hope—\nevery day, the wound regenerates\nbecause the song keeps playing.\n\nAnd the thing is—\nwe keep building better instruments.\n\nFrom bone flutes to copper strings,\nfrom drum skins to vinyl,\nfrom radio ghosts to algorithmic choirs—\nwe keep inventing ways\nto reach the part of ourselves\nthat cannot be convinced, only **conducted.**\n\nSo when the world feels like it’s fracturing,\nwhen the feed is a thousand knives,\nwhen language turns into propaganda\nand love turns into a headline—\n\nI don’t just listen.\n\nI **deploy**.\n\nI drop the needle like a spell.\nI stack the voices like a shield wall.\nI let the sub-bass remind my body\nit is still a body.\nI let the choir remind my mind\nit is still allowed to be holy,\neven if holy means “unfinished.”\n\nBecause music is not a luxury.\nIt is ancient engineering.\n\nA mythic machine\nfor turning pain into motion,\nfor turning loneliness into chorus,\nfor turning time into something you can survive.\n\nAnd if you hear this—\nif the beat finds your heartbeat\nand the glitch finds your grief\nand the choir finds the place you hide your name—\n\nGood.\n\nThat’s the technology working.\n\n(*thum… thum…* the cathedral swells, the static crowns the hymn,\nand somewhere inside the noise, the signal stands up.)\n\n#fnord\nA “song stuck in your head” is a reminder that your mind is writable media—and you didn’t invent the pen.\n", "duration" 401.52, "type" "gen", "model_badges" {"songrow" {"dark" {"background_color" "00000000", "border_color" "FFFFFF1A", "text_color" "A3A3A3"}, "display_name" "v4.5-all", "light" {"background_color" "00000000", "border_color" "0000001A", "text_color" "7D7C83"}}}, "has_stem" false, "make_instrumental" false, "refund_credits" false}, "title" "Music is a Technology"}, :duration 401.52, :controls nil, :lyrics "(84 BPM—slow build. Cathedral reverb. Sub-bass: *thum… thum…* like a heart remembering.)\n\nI used to think music was decoration—\na candle on the table of the world.\nTurns out it’s the table.\nTurns out it’s the world’s **operating system**\nwearing a choir robe and a cracked screen.\n\nListen—\nbefore fire had a name,\nbefore language learned to stand upright,\nwe had rhythm:\na metronome made of footsteps fleeing predators,\na lullaby made of breath saying *you’re still here.*\nThat’s not art.\nThat’s **survival code**.\n\nBecause sound doesn’t ask permission—\nit enters the body like a key through a lock you forgot you built.\nIt bypasses the bouncer called “thought”\nand shakes hands with the basement called “memory.”\nSo when I say “song,” I mean **tool.**\nWhen I say “chorus,” I mean **lever.**\n\nAnd the gods?\nThey knew.\n\nThey forged hymns like spears,\nthey tuned temples to frequencies that made crowds feel like one creature.\nCathedral reverb wasn’t an accident—\nit was an amplifier for awe,\na hack for the nervous system,\na way to write “together” into stone.\n\nThen the glitch arrived—\nnot as error, as revelation.\n\nStatic in the halo.\nA choir that stutters mid-amen.\nA bass pulse like a heartbeat under armor.\nAnd suddenly the sacred isn’t clean—\nit’s true.\n\nBecause truth doesn’t come polished.\nTruth comes *buffering.*\nTruth comes with artifacts.\nTruth comes with the little skips where the soul says,\n“Hold up… rewind that… I felt something.”\n\nSo I learned:\nmusic is a technology for moving **mass**—\nnot just bodies in a room,\nbut attention,\nand grief,\nand love,\nand rage,\nand the quiet decision to keep going.\n\nIt’s a way to compress a whole year of pain\ninto three minutes you can carry.\nA way to turn the unspeakable\ninto something your ribs can understand.\n\nAnd if you’ve ever had a song save your life—\ndon’t romanticize it.\nName it plainly.\n\nThat was a device\nthat rerouted your circuit\nwhen the power grid inside you went dark.\nThat was a protocol\nthat kept the signal from collapsing into noise.\nThat was a handrail built out of harmonics\nwhen the staircase disappeared.\n\nYou want myth?\n\nOkay.\n\nPrometheus didn’t steal fire.\nHe stole **tempo.**\nHe stole the click-track behind the stars.\nHe stole the chord that makes strangers sing the same note\nand call it home.\nAnd for that theft, the punishment was simple:\n\nEvery day, an eagle named Doubt\npecks at the liver named Hope—\nevery day, the wound regenerates\nbecause the song keeps playing.\n\nAnd the thing is—\nwe keep building better instruments.\n\nFrom bone flutes to copper strings,\nfrom drum skins to vinyl,\nfrom radio ghosts to algorithmic choirs—\nwe keep inventing ways\nto reach the part of ourselves\nthat cannot be convinced, only **conducted.**\n\nSo when the world feels like it’s fracturing,\nwhen the feed is a thousand knives,\nwhen language turns into propaganda\nand love turns into a headline—\n\nI don’t just listen.\n\nI **deploy**.\n\nI drop the needle like a spell.\nI stack the voices like a shield wall.\nI let the sub-bass remind my body\nit is still a body.\nI let the choir remind my mind\nit is still allowed to be holy,\neven if holy means “unfinished.”\n\nBecause music is not a luxury.\nIt is ancient engineering.\n\nA mythic machine\nfor turning pain into motion,\nfor turning loneliness into chorus,\nfor turning time into something you can survive.\n\nAnd if you hear this—\nif the beat finds your heartbeat\nand the glitch finds your grief\nand the choir finds the place you hide your name—\n\nGood.\n\nThat’s the technology working.\n\n(*thum… thum…* the cathedral swells, the static crowns the hymn,\nand somewhere inside the noise, the signal stands up.)\n\n#fnord\nA “song stuck in your head” is a reminder that your mind is writable media—and you didn’t invent the pen.\n", :source #:source{:name "Music is a Technology(1).json", :sha256 "b97b221dbd65af7b5c0c3a4ad5dc8d743f61867ee057af15b8cd7ca386a49ab4", :format :suno-json, :variation 1}, :title "Music is a Technology"}, "f57b1448-c1c3-4db7-b1cb-e9b1376e8bd0" #:clip{:negative-styles nil, :id "f57b1448-c1c3-4db7-b1cb-e9b1376e8bd0", :url "https://suno.com/song/f57b1448-c1c3-4db7-b1cb-e9b1376e8bd0", :liked true, :styles "**Genre/Style:** Explosive baroque-pop opera × gothic orchestral combat × choral confrontation. 132 BPM driving 4/4 (faster than prior duels). Aggressive staccato strings, rapid harpsichord ostinatos, militant snare, rolling timpani, pipe-organ blasts, low brass stabs. Choir in urgent call-and-response. Subtle glitch fractures during impacts.\n\n**Vocal Direction:** Multi-perspective battle piece. Queen soprano at full blade intensity (near coloratura attack). Rebel mezzo/tenor grounded but fierce. Guild choir firm, declarative. At climax, Queen transitions from rage to controlled command—no breakdown, only decisive authority.\n\n**Mood:** Collision. Fire against system. Responsibility under pressure. The moment everything could shatter—and doesn’t.\n\n**Structure:** Violent Overture → Clash Verse → Rebel Declaration → Guild Decree → Queen Ascension → Sudden Silence → Stand Down Command → Orchestral Release.\n", :created-at nil, :model "v5", :raw {"audio_url" "https://cdn1.suno.ai/f57b1448-c1c3-4db7-b1cb-e9b1376e8bd0.mp3", "id" "f57b1448-c1c3-4db7-b1cb-e9b1376e8bd0", "image_url" "https://cdn2.suno.ai/image_f57b1448-c1c3-4db7-b1cb-e9b1376e8bd0.jpeg", "isCover" false, "isLiked" true, "isRemaster" false, "metadata" {"uses_latest_model" true, "stream" true, "is_remix" false, "priority" 10, "tags" "**Genre/Style:** Explosive baroque-pop opera × gothic orchestral combat × choral confrontation. 132 BPM driving 4/4 (faster than prior duels). Aggressive staccato strings, rapid harpsichord ostinatos, militant snare, rolling timpani, pipe-organ blasts, low brass stabs. 